// @no-instantiate
// ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES — the GENERIC rung of the inline-literal-union element family.
// `vl check` rc 0, `vl check --codegen` reports `the emitted module is not valid wasm … type
// mismatch: expected (ref $type), found i32`, and the engine refuses the module at load.
//
// It is what remains of `xfail-miscompile-inline-litunion-element-read.vl` after that file's
// PLAIN-FUNCTION rung graduated. That rung closed because the RETURN boundary grew the arm it
// lacked: `retStrWiden` fired for `: string`, for `string | null` and for an inferred
// `string`, and an INLINE literal-union return (`: "a" | "b"`) was claimed by none of them,
// while the NAMED form (`: K`) is claimed by `retLitUnion` and correctly does not widen
// because it reps as the atom.
//
// THE FIX WAS PER-BOUNDARY, WHICH IS WHY THIS SURVIVED IT. Each boundary decides its slot type
// in its own ladder, so the generic INSTANCE's boundary — `pick$m1`, whose `T` is bound to the
// inline union — is untouched. Same disagreement underneath: `ctxKeepsLitUnion` PRESERVES a
// literal union's members at `RC_ELEM` and SOFTENS them at `RC_ROOT` / `RC_FN_PARAM`, so
// `("a" | "b")[]` is an array of interned i32 ATOMS while `T` resolved to `"a" | "b"` in the
// result position is a string reference.
//
// THE DECLARED ALIAS IS THE CONTROL AND IT IS GREEN — replace the inline `("a" | "b")` with a
// `type K = "a" | "b"` throughout and this runs. The trigger is the INLINE spelling, not the
// literal-union family.
//
// A SILENT SIBLING, WORSE AND STILL UNPINNABLE — a BINDING rather than a return or a generic:
//
//   const tags: ("a" | "b")[] = ["b", "a"]
//   const v: "a" | "b" = tags[0]
//   print(v)                              // prints `0`, the raw atom id
//
// compiles, VALIDATES, runs, exits 0 and prints a wrong VALUE. No fixture kind pins that
// (`@no-instantiate` cannot pin a valid module and `@trap` explicitly rejects it) — the gap
// `closures/permuted-object-closure-arms.vl` also records. Its ladder is keyed on
// `letAnnNm == "string"`, which an inline literal-union annotation does not spell.
//
// The real close for both is `ctxKeepsLitUnion` picking ONE position-independent rule; its own
// header states the policy, names the two POSITION rules that were built and REFUTED, and says
// why the spelling must be position-independent (the union registry is keyed by NAME and blind
// to position, so a rule that spells one arena type two ways mints two rows for one type).
// Filed with its diff in `docs/internals/destringify-types-program.md` (2026-07-29).
//
// The directive is bare: the two engines that run this corpus word the rejection differently
// and share no substring, so this pins the FACT (the module does not instantiate) and leaves
// the wording to the engine.
function pick<T>(self: T[]): T { self[0] }

const tags: ("a" | "b")[] = ["b", "a", "b"]
print(pick(tags))
